WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
It's a new day on SpaceX Daily, episode 40.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:08.000
It's July 21, 2026, and I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.780
From the launch pad to the trading floor,

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.600
here's everything that moved today.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.560
Northrop Grumman's robot-armed satellite

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.600
heads to orbit today on a Falcon 9, moving on orbit

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.940
servicing from study to flight hardware.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.740
The mission lifts off from Cape Canaveral

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.000
carrying Northrop Grumman's two-handed robotic satellite

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:28.900
repair system.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.840
The payload tests autonomous grappling

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.560
and servicing techniques in low Earth orbit.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.700
SpaceX supplies the Falcon 9 booster and upper stage

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.680
for the ride to the target altitude.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.140
The flight adds another data point

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.720
on pad turnaround times at the Florida site.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.060
This launch continues the Falcon 9 reuse program's focus

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.480
on rapid turnaround after each mission,

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.060
directly addressing the open question

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.740
of how many flights a single booster can sustain

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.620
before major refurbishment.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.380
Observers will next watch whether the same first stage

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.720
returns to flight within the compressed intervals

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:09.420
already demonstrated at Cape Canaveral in prior weeks.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.760
Internal records show Falcon 9 completed more orbital missions

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.420
in 2025 than any prior year.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.400
The data track booster flight counts turn around intervals

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.160
and payload deployment success rates across both coasts.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.080
The metrics quantify how reuse cycles

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.200
and pad throughput scaled together.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.700
Falcon launch cadence and reuse

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.120
reached record levels in 2025.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.220
Today's release of those consolidated numbers

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.860
supplies the first public baseline against which

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.760
2026 flight rate targets can be measured

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.960
and directly informs the open question

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.680
of annual launch rate ceiling.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.140
The next data release will show whether the same reuse

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.580
intervals hold when both Florida and California pads

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.060
operate at simultaneous high cadence.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.440
The company announced its first post IPO earnings release,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.680
which automatically ends the initial 30-day lockup

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:04.700
for early shareholders.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.080
Millions of shares become eligible for sale

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:09.820
once the quiet period lifts.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.860
The filing gives investors the first public view

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.640
of quarterly revenue split between launch services

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:16.560
and starlink.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.460
SPCX listed on NASDAQ June 12th, 2026.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.500
The upcoming earnings report will mark the first

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.820
quarterly update under public company disclosure rules

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.260
and will test how the dual class structure

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:33.480
affects trading volume once the lockup window opens.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.620
Groups filed objections to a proposed exchange

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.400
of parcels near Boca Chica

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.220
that would expand SpaceX controlled acreage.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.660
The swap involves federal land currently managed

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.040
for wildlife habitat.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.060
Regulators must now weigh public comments

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:50.800
before any transfer proceeds.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.820
The outcome directly affects buffer zones

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.360
around the star-based production and launch site.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.780
Star-based infrastructure readiness remains a tracked

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.860
limiter on Starship flight rate.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.920
Any delay in securing additional buffer land

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.920
would push back the timeline for expanding the gigabay

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.480
and mechazilla tower operations already under construction.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.320
Federal agencies will next decide whether the objections

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.480
alter the acreage transfer schedule.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.320
Observers on X noted that a sitting congressman

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.080
who had avoided all stock trades for six years

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.900
purchased SPCX shares shortly after the IPO.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.280
Separate threads discussed whether the landswap

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.280
objections could delay star-based expansion permits

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.720
already under review.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.500
Several accounts shared photos of the Cape Canaveral pad

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:42.820
being ready for the Northrop Grumman payload integration.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:45.860
Independent analysts posted timelines

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:48.860
comparing the 2025 Falcon 9 flight rate

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.640
against historical records from other operators.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.280
Worth keeping an eye on, conservation groups argue

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.460
the proposed federal land exchange near Boca Chica

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:02.020
would remove protected habitat without adequate mitigation.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.960
The filings claim the swap violates

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:06.800
existing environmental agreements

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.240
tied to earlier star-based approvals.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.500
Resolution now rests with federal agencies

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.540
weighing the objections against SPCX's stated need

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.320
for additional buffer acreage.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:19.640
Any ruling that blocks or conditions

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.560
the transfer would require SPCX to pursue alternative acreage

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:26.360
or redesign buffer plans already incorporated

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.340
into star-based master scheduling.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.200
On the AI front, cursor-published analysis showing

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.420
that agent swarms built from a high-performance planner

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.100
paired with a low-cost execution model

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.840
shift the economics of large inference workloads.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.760
The approach reduces per token cost

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.560
while maintaining output quality

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.440
on coding and planning tasks.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.100
The same planner execution split could be applied

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.200
to on-orbit inference tasks

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:57.020
once star-link laser links connect future orbital accelerators

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.040
to ground users.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:03.080
Elon Musk's Colossus 2 site drew fresh regulatory scrutiny

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:06.140
after reports detailed 59 gas turbines

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.880
installed without prior federal air permits.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.540
The turbines were added to meet immediate power demand

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.420
for the expanded training cluster.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.840
Power scaling at Colossus directly determines

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.460
how quickly XAI can complete the next groc training run

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.560
and whether excess capacity can later support

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:26.980
SpaceX orbital compute experiments.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.340
From an engineering standpoint,

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.520
the Falcon 9 reuse loop replaces the cost

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.240
of building a new first stage

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.060
with the far smaller expense of propellant, ground support

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:40.020
and targeted part replacements.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.980
Each cycle recovers the booster, inspects the engines

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.380
and tanks, then flies again with minimal refurbishment.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.800
The data show that booster flight counts climbed

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.900
while average turnaround time fell,

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.900
indicating that the marginal cost per mission

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:00.280
approached the raw inputs of RP1, liquid oxygen and helium.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:03.860
When the marginal cost per flight falls close

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.440
to those raw inputs, the idiot index,

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.480
the ratio of finished launch price

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.720
to the sum of propellant and basic materials drops sharply,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.840
revealing how reuse compresses

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.860
the traditional aerospace markup.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.520
That cost curve matters because the same hardware

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.560
now supports both commercial starlink deployments

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.920
and the Northrop Grumman servicing mission.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:29.560
When a booster flies its 10th or 15th mission,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.000
the per flight amortization of the original vehicle

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.320
drops below the price of a single new Merlin engine set.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:39.800
The remaining variable is how many additional flights

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.960
the Octa web structure and turbo pumps can sustain

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.060
before inspection intervals lengthen or parts must be swapped.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.440
Historical parallels exist in container shipping

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:52.960
where standardized steel boxes moved

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.880
from single use crates to hundreds of reuses.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:00.140
Falcon 9 applies the same logic to the first stage airframe,

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.500
trading one time certification cost

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.020
for repeated flight cycles.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:08.220
The next measurable step is whether the 2026 cadence

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.320
can repeat the 2025 pattern while two pads

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:13.120
on each coast stay active.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.860
Any extension of booster life limits

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.460
or further compression of post flight processing

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:21.600
directly widens the gap between raw material cost

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:23.580
and delivered payload to orbit.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:26.460
Engineers will therefore watch the next set

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.460
of post flight inspections for signs

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:31.520
that the current inspection cadence can be safely shortened

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.580
without increasing the risk of in-flight anomalies.

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:40.740
And a quick market note, SPCX is at $126.75,

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.340
up 5.4% versus the previous close.

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:48.000
Before we go, the next set of post flight inspections

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.500
on the returning Falcon 9 booster will show

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:53.080
whether turnaround intervals can compress further.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.840
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:05.220
SPCX is trading at $126.75, up 5.4%.

